ngm:a-star-algorithm a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:comment "The A* algorithm is an informed best-first graph-search method that finds a least-cost path from a start node to a goal node by evaluating each candidate node n with the function f(n) = g(n) + h(n), where g(n) is the exact cost accumulated from the start node to n and h(n) is an admissible heuristic estimating the remaining cost from n to the goal. By always expanding the open-list node with the lowest f-value, A* is guaranteed to find the optimal path when h is admissible (never overestimates) and is also consistent (satisfies the triangle inequality h(n) ≤ c(n,n') + h(n') for every edge (n,n')). A* generalises Dijkstra's algorithm by adding goal-directed heuristic guidance, and subsumes greedy best-first search as the special case g(n)=0. First described by Peter Hart, Nils Nilsson, and Bertram Raphael at the Stanford Research Institute in 1968, A* remains the most widely deployed optimal graph-search algorithm in artificial intelligence, robotics, game development, and logistics."@en ;
    rdfs:label "A Star Algorithm"@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:graph-search ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:informed-search ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ngm:search-algorithm ;
